CODE EXPLAINED
1. Import libraries
   pandas handles tabular data; matplotlib creates graphs; load_wine loads the built-in dataset; StandardScaler standardizes features; PCA performs dimensionality reduction.

2. wine = load_wine(as_frame=True)
   Loads the Wine dataset as a pandas-friendly object. It contains chemical measurements of wine samples.

3. X, y = wine.data, wine.target
   X contains the input features and y contains the wine class labels.

4. print(X.head())
   Displays the first five rows of the feature dataset.

5. X = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X)
   Standardizes each feature to mean 0 and standard deviation 1. Scaling matters because PCA is affected by feature magnitudes.

6. pca = PCA(n_components=2)
   Creates a PCA model that keeps two principal components.

7. result = pca.fit_transform(X)
   Learns the principal components and transforms the original 13 features into two new features, PC1 and PC2.

8. print("PCA Result:", result)
   Displays the transformed two-dimensional data.

9. pca.explained_variance_ratio_
   Shows the proportion of total variance explained by each principal component.

10. sum(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)
    Calculates the total proportion of variance retained by the two components.

11. X.shape[1] and result.shape[1]
    Display the original feature count (13) and reduced feature count (2).

12. plt.scatter(result[:, 0], result[:, 1], c=y)
    Plots PC1 against PC2. The colour represents the wine class.

13. plt.xlabel(), plt.ylabel(), plt.title(), plt.show()
    Add labels and a title, then display the scatter plot.

PIPELINE:
Load Wine dataset -> separate features and labels -> standardize features -> apply PCA -> display explained variance -> plot the two principal components.

